diff --git a/docs/collector-input-limits.md b/docs/collector-input-limits.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7c05a31
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/collector-input-limits.md
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+# Collector input limits
+
+The collector now requires four positive integer settings in addition to its
+listener and credential configuration. Missing or invalid values stop startup.
+These limits affect dashboard telemetry only; they do not change efsn consensus,
+block validity, mining, or eligibility to join the chain.
+
+| Setting | Enforced behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| `COLLECTOR_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` | Incoming messages must be strictly below this byte threshold, including their JSON envelope. Applies to `/api`, `/primus` and `/external`, including aggregate fragmented messages. Compression is disabled. |
+| `COLLECTOR_MAX_CONNECTIONS` | Native HTTP server TCP connection cap, shared across all paths and incomplete upgrades. Connections above it are dropped. A slot becomes reusable after the server observes TCP closure. |
+| `COLLECTOR_HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS` | `/api` must complete credential checking and registration within this interval after its WebSocket connection. Expiry ends the session and releases any identity reservation. |
+| `COLLECTOR_MESSAGES_PER_SECOND` | Each connection starts with this many application-message credits. Credits refill continuously at this rate, capped at the initial allowance. An event needs one whole credit; exceeding the allowance ends the session. |
+
+Settings accept decimal integers from 1 to 2147483647. The local fixtures use
+65536 bytes, 16 connections, 1000 ms and 100 messages/second. These are fixture
+values, not production sizing. Measure complete real efsn block/history reports,
+sync bursts, expected operator count and proxy overhead before choosing deployed
+limits. A legitimate report above a configured limit will also disconnect.
+In particular, a history report includes transaction hashes for up to 50 blocks.
+
+The locked `ws` 1.1.5 receiver rejects the exact byte threshold too. Tests cover
+2047 accepted bytes versus 2048 rejected bytes, multibyte text and fragmented
+messages. Transport/JSON decoding errors destroy the request's socket promptly;
+this avoids the old library's 30-second close-handshake wait. Peers then observe
+abnormal closure code 1006. Ordinary session rejection still uses Primus closure.
+
+Application envelopes are checked before the legacy event plugin runs. They
+must contain exactly an event name and an object argument. `/api` accepts hello,
+update, block, pending, stats, history, node-ping and latency; existing credential
+and session-ownership checks still apply. `/primus` accepts ready and client-pong.
+The unused legacy `/external` stream accepts no inbound application events.
+Old JavaScript clients sending a separate `_latency` packet are outside this
+efsn-focused contract; efsn uses the authenticated `latency` event.
+
+History replies are limited to 50 entries, matching efsn's default batch and
+this collector's requested list size. Individual report fields are otherwise
+still subject to the existing validation, not a new full telemetry schema.
+The limiter does not count Primus control messages, WebSocket control frames or
+HTTP traffic. The local proxy still needs request, handshake, connection-rate
+and public-traffic limits. Application limits reset with a new connection;
+they are not a source-address quota or protection against many reconnects.
+
+## Acceptance and outstanding work
+
+Node 22.11.0 passes 120 contracts and eight real loopback socket scenarios with
+synthetic credentials. Tests cover login expiry, late-registration rejection,
+input/rate/connection limits, fragmented messages, invalid envelopes, reconnects,
+and normal head/history reporting followed by an oversized history batch.
+Processes exit and test sockets close. Existing dependency deprecations remain.
+This step changes no dependency versions or frontend/storage code.
+
+The inherited chart history remains a separate blocker. Its partly filled cache
+can ignore an advancing head. With head 100 and history 51–100, it stores all 50
+heights but charts 51–90 rather than the latest 40. Per-height fork arrays also
+lack a retention cap. These findings need focused correction and acceptance;
+passing ingress checks does not prove bounded total process memory or correct
+chart reporting. Outbound buffering/slow consumers and credential-count sizing
+also remain part of resource review.
+
+Next distinguish collector connection state from recent accepted block, stats
+and pending reports. Neither a fresh database snapshot nor a ping proves those
+fields are recent. Then complete real efsn/RPC comparison, chart/cache bounds,
+runtime/dependency review, supervision and TLS/proxy acceptance before public use.
